      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e \u003e looks like commit is little confusing I will update it.\n\u003e \u003e 1. Yes device wont enter low power mode in both the cases\n\u003e Then should a similar fix be applied to tc_unattached_snk_run?\n\nI think we can ignore drp_state[port] \u003d\u003d PD_DRP_TOGGLE_ON state and check only \ncc_is_open(cc1, cc2) so that we enter LPM for all the cases.\n\n\u003e \u003e 2. CONFIG_USB_PD_DUAL_ROLE_AUTO_TOGGLE is not supported by IT83xx \u0026 FUSB302.\n\u003e What I meant is this: What does a TCPC like that do in LPM? How should the TCPC or the EC decide to exit LPM in that case?\n\nWe want pd_task to be in wait state in LPM. If TCPC chip supports hardware based LPM it will enter LPM. Upon any device connect, TCPC chip comes back to active state and also pd_task comes out of wait state\n\n\u003e \u003e 3. Once SOC is in active state PD_DRP_TOGGLE_ON is set hence even when no device is connected TCPC wont go into low power mode\n\u003e And you\u0027re saying this is the desired behavior for a TCPC that doesn\u0027t support DRP AutoToggle?\n\nThis is the bug we found, this CL keeps the TCPC in LPM even when PD_DRP_TOGGLE_ON in SOC active state.\n\n\n\u003e I think I might have some idea of what you\u0027re getting at: For a TCPC that doesn\u0027t support DRP AutoToggle, the only appropriate time to go into LPM is when the AP is off. Because it doesn\u0027t toggle in LPM, it has no way to get out of LPM by itself. Therefore, this change doesn\u0027t or shouldn\u0027t let it enter LPM once the AP is on.\n\u003e \n\u003e Is that close to an accurate summary of the point of this change?\n\n\nThis is current code logic and it looks like its a bug. We are modifying it to so that TCPC chip enters LPM even when SOC is on (PD_DRP_TOGGLE_ON)",
